/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  estimate_acetone
  ================

  The FIRST stage of the props lifecycle (CREATE -> curate -> wire -> simulate):
  build a pure-component property set from its molecular GROUPS by Joback
  group contribution, and SEE each group's contribution add up.

  Acetone CH3-CO-CH3  =  2 x CH3  +  1 x ketone (>C=O).

  The reference block below validates FOUR numbers -- Tb, Tc, Pc, omega -- and
  no others: Joback lands 2.2 % low on Tb, 1.5 % low on Tc, 2.2 % high on Pc
  (read off the golden against the block, 2026-09-02), and you SEE the error,
  the whole point of "estimation, then trust".  This header used to claim
  "~2 % on Tc/Pc/Vc and ~0.3 % on dHf"; the block carries no reference Vc and
  no reference dHf, so those two were claims nothing in the case checks.
  omega comes from (Tb,Tc,Pc) by Lee-Kesler, not a group sum.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        estimate_acetone;
        type        estimateComponent;
        component   acetone;
        groups
        (
            { group CH3;     count 2; }
            { group ketone;  count 1; }
        );
        reference
        {
            source  "NIST WebBook / Poling et al.";
            Tb      329.2 K;
            Tc      508.1 K;
            Pc      47.0  bar;
            omega   0.307;
        }
        // Opt-in PROMOTE: write a component proposal .dat from the estimate.
        // `auto` => constant/components/<component>.estimated.dat -- ONE
        // stable proposal, replaced per run (EstimateComponent.cpp), a
        // glass-box, reviewable file (ready fields active, gaps as TODOs);
        // promote with `mv` once the gaps are filled.  The file NAMES its
        // own remaining gaps rather than this comment guessing them: the
        // run now fills Vliq (Rackett/Yamada-Gunn) and Psat
        // (Ambrose-Walton), so on acetone only s_298 is left -- and this
        // line used to list all three, plus a dated filename the engine
        // stopped writing.
        output  { proposal auto; }
    }
);

/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE VIEW THE LESSON OPENS ON (DEV.md 4c C443).

  The lesson: build a pure component from its molecular groups (Joback: two CH3 and one ketone) and see the error: estimation, then trust.

  The plot: what the group sum returned for acetone: the normal boiling point, the critical temperature and pressure and the acentric factor, the four numbers the case holds against reference values.

  Declared over what the operations already publish; it computes nothing.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

views
(
    {
        name    estimated_properties;
        type    table;
        title   "What the Joback groups give for acetone";
        note    "Compare these with the reference block of this case (NIST WebBook / Poling et al.: Tb 329.2 K, Tc 508.1 K, Pc 47.0 bar, omega 0.307): Joback lands about 2 % low on the boiling point and the critical temperature and 2 % high on the critical pressure. The acentric factor is not a group sum; it comes from the other three by the Lee-Kesler relation.";
        columns
        (
            { name Tb; label "normal boiling point"; unit "K"; }
            { name Tc; label "critical temperature"; unit "K"; }
            { name Pc; label "critical pressure"; unit "bar"; }
            { name omega; label "acentric factor"; unit "-"; }
        );
        rows
        (
            { name estimate; label "Joback estimate"; cells { Tb { op estimate_acetone; key Tb_K; } Tc { op estimate_acetone; key Tc_K; } Pc { op estimate_acetone; key Pc_bar; } omega { op estimate_acetone; key omega; } } }
        );
    }
);
